<Prose title="Support" metaTitle="Gigaflush support: install help, bugs and FAQs" description="Get help with Gigaflush: get past the macOS “can’t verify developer” and Windows SmartScreen prompts, report bugs, and read answers to common questions.">
  <h2>Download a new version</h2>
  <p>Get the latest build for any platform from the <a href="/#download">download section</a>, or browse every version on <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/releases`}>GitHub Releases</a>. Install it over the old one; your settings carry over.</p>

  <h2>It won't open</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>macOS says it can't verify the developer.</strong> The app isn't signed by Apple. Open it once, then go to System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security and click <em>Open Anyway</em>. Or run <code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Gigaflush.app</code> in Terminal.</li>
    <li><strong>macOS says the app is damaged.</strong> That's the same download flag; run the <code>xattr</code> command above.</li>
    <li><strong>Windows SmartScreen blocks the installer.</strong> Click <em>More info</em>, then <em>Run anyway</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Linux: no tray icon.</strong> Install <code>libayatana-appindicator3-1</code>, and on GNOME the AppIndicator extension.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Bugs and feature requests</h2>
  <p>Please <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/issues/new`}>open an issue on GitHub</a> with your OS version, Gigaflush version (shown at the bottom of the "How it works" sheet), and what you expected to happen.</p>

  <h2>Anything private</h2>
  <p>Email <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A folder I expected isn't listed.</strong> Gigaflush only lists folders that sit beside a project file, such as <code>node_modules</code> next to <code>package.json</code>. Also check that its type is enabled under Cleanup types.</li>
    <li><strong>A folder couldn't be removed.</strong> Stop dev servers, watchers, and IDE indexers that may hold files open, then try again.</li>
    <li><strong>Sizes don't match the space I got back.</strong> Sizes are logical file sizes; compression, clones, and hard links can make the real savings differ.</li>
  </ul>
</Prose>
